# What `docker build .` and `docker compose up --build` send to the builder.
#
# Everything is left out, and then exactly what the two Dockerfiles copy is let
# back in. Without this file the whole directory went along on every build:
# dashboard/node_modules (127 MB), scripts/node_modules, .git, and on a machine
# that has been running for a while `backups/` with the database dumps in it
# and `.env` with the passwords. None of that is read by the build, and an
# install whose builder is remote would upload it.
#
# The dashboard's node_modules also got into the image itself: `COPY dashboard/`
# followed `npm ci` and laid the builder's own packages over the ones just
# installed for the image's platform. The Dockerfile now names its sources one
# by one, so that no longer depends on this file; this file is what keeps the
# 127 MB off the wire.
#
# Matching follows Docker's rules, not git's: a pattern is anchored at the root
# of the context, `**` crosses directories, and the last matching line wins.
# https://docs.docker.com/build/concepts/context/#dockerignore-files
# tests/test_build_context.py reads this file with those rules and fails if a
# file a Dockerfile copies is left out, or if node_modules, .git, .env or a
# backup can get in.
*

# The product image (Dockerfile). Not scripts/: those are the developer's
# tools, run from a checkout and never inside the product container (the
# Dockerfile says why, next to where it used to copy them).
!requirements/requirements.lock.txt
!alembic
!VERSION
!LICENSE
!NOTICE
!korely_agent
!deploy/entrypoint.sh
!dashboard
# The terms of the local embedder's model, put next to its weights.
!licenses

# The test image (Dockerfile.dev).
!requirements/requirements-dev.lock.txt

# Inside what came back in, what is never source. After the lines above, so
# that it wins.
**/node_modules
**/__pycache__
**/*.pyc
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.DS_Store
dashboard/dist
dashboard/.env
dashboard/.env.local
